<resources>
    <!--
      The window's own background, i.e. the one frame before Compose exists.
      It is the ordinary void tone; MainActivity re-points it at the AMOLED
      black at runtime, because a static resource cannot follow the toggle
      (this window used to be pure black, which put a black surface under
      every screen at launch - not just Home, the app's one black screen).
    -->
    <style name="Theme.KBStream" parent="android:Theme.Material.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:colorBackground">#0A0E14</item>
    </style>

    <!--
      The launch theme: the branded frame the system paints between the
      launcher and this app's first composed pixel.

      Only ever that frame. MainActivity swaps back to Theme.KBStream before
      super.onCreate, so nothing after the starting window inherits the
      drawable background - the Compose splash (LaunchSplash) owns every beat
      from there on, including the handoff.

      Static and void-toned on purpose: AMOLED is a runtime, profile-scoped
      toggle that a drawable cannot follow, and this window is drawn before the
      profile has even been read. AMOLED users see one void beat instead of
      pure black, which is the same tone this window painted before there was a
      splash at all.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.KBStream.Splash" parent="Theme.KBStream">
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@drawable/splash_background</item>
    </style>
</resources>
